      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "The purposes of this prospective non-randomized study were to confirm the feasibility of the biodegradable sub-acromial spacer (InSpace™) implantation in patients with massive irreparable rotator cuff tear and to determine the safety profile and functional results 3 years post-implantation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Twenty patients were implanted with the InSpace™ device and assessed up to 3 years of post-implantation. Improvement in shoulder function was assessed using Constant score, while ease of use of the system was recorded by surgeons as were device-related adverse events."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Twenty patients were available for assessment. Implantation was performed arthroscopically in all patients, and a range of deployment time was 2-20 min. The mean total Constant score increased from 33.4 to 65.4 points at 3 years. There was an improvement of 6.4 points in subjective pain score which commenced at 1 week post-operatively and was sustained until 3 years of follow-up. Also activities of daily living and motions commenced improvement by 9.4 and 7.7 points, respectively. Improvement in power was only evident at 18 months of follow-up but was sustained at 3 years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Arthroscopic deployment of a co-polymer biodegradable spacer (balloon) into the sub-acromial space for an irreparable rotator cuff tear was found to be low-risk and simple procedure associated with improvement in shoulder function and low rate of complications."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "LEVEL OF EVIDENCE",
          "content": "IV; therapeutic case series."
        }
      ]},
